# Game audio: synthesised NES-style SFX and music

All sound effects and music are generated by a Python script. No audio is
recorded, sampled or extracted from anywhere.

## Rebuild

```sh
cd /path/to/repo
python3 tools/audio/build_audio.py                 # render, encode, write audio.json, verify
python3 tools/audio/build_audio.py --no-verify     # skip the checks
python3 tools/audio/build_audio.py --plots /tmp/audio_check   # also write spectrogram PNGs
python3 tools/audio/verify_audio.py [plot_dir]     # re-run only the checks
```

Requirements: Python 3.10+, `numpy`, `scipy`, and an `ffmpeg` built with
`libvorbis` and `libmp3lame`. The script looks for `$FFMPEG`, then
`/hutian/.mamba/bin/ffmpeg`, then `ffmpeg` on `PATH`. The checks optionally use
`soundfile` (libvorbis) to get exact OGG lengths, and `matplotlib` for the plots.
A build takes about 40 s and is deterministic: two runs produce byte-identical
files, because the OGG and MP3 are written with ffmpeg's `+bitexact` flags.

Outputs (paths relative to `assets/`):

| path | content |
|---|---|
| `audio/sfx/<id>.wav`, `audio/sfx/<id>.ogg` | SFX, 44.1 kHz mono 16-bit WAV + Vorbis q5 |
| `audio/music/<id>.ogg`, `audio/music/<id>.mp3` | music, Vorbis q5 + MP3 128 kbit/s |
| `audio/audio.json` | manifest: files, duration, loop, loopStart, loopEnd |

